53
00:04:58,391 --> 00:05:05,196
[SPEAKER_02]: And that, I mean, I don't mean like it exists after me or before me, because that would be, again, that external perception of the world independent of me.

54
00:05:05,256 --> 00:05:05,376
[SPEAKER_02]: No.

55
00:05:05,956 --> 00:05:12,881
[SPEAKER_02]: A present, presently enduring feelings, for example, presently enduring thoughts or a mood, it's real as such in its own domain.

56
00:05:14,622 --> 00:05:20,505
[SPEAKER_02]: Because if it's not real as such, if it's not perceived, how do you know what mood you're in?

57
00:05:20,525 --> 00:05:21,806
[SPEAKER_02]: How do you know what thoughts you have?

58
00:05:21,906 --> 00:05:22,707
[SPEAKER_02]: You are perceiving it.

59
00:05:23,764 --> 00:05:41,028
[SPEAKER_02]: It's just that, obviously, through non-restraint, pursuit of sensuality, development of all the wrong views towards... on the basis of perverted order of sense organs and sense bases... sorry, sense objects and sense bases, you sort of... you dismiss this.

60
00:05:41,168 --> 00:05:42,088
[SPEAKER_02]: You just see past it.

61
00:05:42,348 --> 00:05:50,050
[SPEAKER_02]: You see past these phenomena that are very real, and actually, they're kind of even more real than the world you assume is real.

62
00:05:50,750 --> 00:05:51,850
[SPEAKER_02]: They're the closest to you.

63
00:05:53,777 --> 00:05:55,959
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's the authenticity we often talk about.

64
00:05:56,939 --> 00:05:57,820
[SPEAKER_02]: Mundane right view.

65
00:05:58,781 --> 00:06:00,222
[SPEAKER_02]: It will lead to the mundane right view.

66
00:06:00,262 --> 00:06:00,622
[SPEAKER_02]: Exactly.

67
00:06:00,822 --> 00:06:01,202
[SPEAKER_02]: Exactly.

68
00:06:01,583 --> 00:06:01,823
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

69
00:06:03,444 --> 00:06:03,944
[SPEAKER_02]: And, eh...

70
00:06:05,185 --> 00:06:06,946
