00:14:36,415 --> 00:14:39,718
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, like, you can only... Like, pick them up or grab them.

182
00:14:39,798 --> 00:14:39,938
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

183
00:14:39,958 --> 00:14:48,765
[SPEAKER_01]: So, so, if you're concerned with beautiful or ugly of particular phenomena, like phenomena within phenomena, and because that's how you get absorbed and how you lose that origin.

184
00:14:48,945 --> 00:14:49,686
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

185
00:14:50,046 --> 00:14:50,726
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that is...

186
00:14:51,027 --> 00:14:54,970
[SPEAKER_01]: So, to be drawn that way is to already not attend from the origin.

187
00:14:55,150 --> 00:14:55,490
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

188
00:14:55,630 --> 00:14:57,652
[SPEAKER_01]: But, when one is attending from the origin,

189
00:14:58,072 --> 00:15:09,376
[SPEAKER_01]: That whole domain is still there, but can you then pick up those signs and features and get lost in that direction while concurrently attending?

190
00:15:09,456 --> 00:15:10,976
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

191
00:15:11,056 --> 00:15:20,040
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why, when attending from the origin, the whole thing begins to cool down, because you keep dwelling on the totality, so to speak, and not lose yourself within it.

192
00:15:20,560 --> 00:15:22,820
[SPEAKER_01]: And losing yourself within it is the fuel for it.

193
00:15:23,161 --> 00:15:27,162
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, but then the key thing is to not own that origin.

194
00:15:28,188 --> 00:15:30,151
[SPEAKER_01]: And say... Yeah, eventually, yeah.

195
00:15:31,353 --> 00:15:40,507
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, you can't... See, if you try to sort of, right away, start not owning it and so on, you would be attending to that without...

196
00:15:41,948 --> 00:15:43,669
[SPEAKER_02]: Without perspective, without yoniso.

197
00:15:43,789 --> 00:15:44,009
[SPEAKER_02]: Yes.

198
00:15:44,089 --> 00:15:45,490
[SPEAKER_02]: It won't be concurrent attention.

199
00:15:45,910 --> 00:15:56,775
[SPEAKER_02]: But, if you're steadied in it, and, obviously, sense restraint and a correct mode of behavior would be supporting that practice of putting things in brackets, or step back.

200
00:15:56,835 --> 00:15:57,175
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

201
00:15:57,556 --> 00:16:00,117
[SPEAKER_02]: And allowing it to endure from what you step back.

202
00:16:00,237 --> 00:16:04,439
[SPEAKER_02]: Not fix, change, psychologize, modify, cover up.

203
00:16:05,379 --> 00:16:08,761
[SPEAKER_02]: Then, yes, you would be, obviously, discerning more and more.

204
00:16:09,061 --> 00:16:10,662
[SPEAKER_02]: So, then, you can you can learn

205
00:16:11,193 --> 00:16:15,536
[SPEAKER_02]: how to now put these more refined aspects into brackets, because they are real as such.

206
00:16:16,256 --> 00:16:23,961
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why I keep saying whatever, you know, like, even if there's like some ambiguous sense feeling or something, oh, the degree of its ambiguity, it's real as such.

207
00:16:24,161 --> 00:16:26,362
[SPEAKER_02]: It does not require clarification in terms of

208
00:16:26,782 --> 00:16:28,663
[SPEAKER_02]: Making it into something more palpable.

209
00:16:29,424 --> 00:16:34,087
[SPEAKER_02]: You realize clarification is on the level of not ignoring even unclear things.

210
00:16:34,567 --> 00:16:35,608
[SPEAKER_02]: This phenomenon is unclear.

211
00:16:36,008 --> 00:16:36,828
[SPEAKER_02]: It is real as such.

212
00:16:37,189 --> 00:16:37,409
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

213
00:16:37,869 --> 00:16:38,750
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's quite clear.

214
00:16:39,290 --> 00:16:43,793
[SPEAKER_01]: And by regarding things this way, man cannot root out doubt.

215
00:16:43,953 --> 00:16:44,293
[SPEAKER_02]: Exactly.

216
00:16:44,313 --> 00:16:47,415
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why the mundane right view developed like this.

217
00:16:47,635 --> 00:16:52,538
[SPEAKER_02]: It's like pretty much paired with sense restraint and everything else would have to result into the right way of practice.

218
00:16:53,439 --> 00:16:55,020
[SPEAKER_02]: Because that's what's lacking.

219
00:16:56,738 --> 00:16:57,559
[SPEAKER_02]: There is instruction.

220
00:16:57,579 --> 00:16:58,359
[SPEAKER_02]: There are suttas.

221
00:16:59,440 --> 00:17:02,721
[SPEAKER_02]: There are people that practice restraint and keep the precepts and so on.

222
00:17:03,442 --> 00:17:08,644
[SPEAKER_02]: But there are not many people that know how to see things to the extent that they have arisen.

223
00:17:09,345 --> 00:17:19,130
[SPEAKER_02]: There are not many people that know that they have to change their attitude and recognize things existing as phenomena independent of their sense of their body and their mind.

224
00:17:19,690 --> 00:17:19,930
[SPEAKER_02]: Both.

225
00:17:21,111 --> 00:17:23,372
[SPEAKER_02]: Just they are given first beforehand.

226
00:17:25,112 --> 00:17:26,814
[SPEAKER_02]: And then you realize, oh, this is first.

227
00:17:26,934 --> 00:17:33,482
[SPEAKER_02]: Whatever's manifested, to the extent it's manifested, is the experience as a whole, is the totality of the experience.

228
00:17:34,464 --> 00:17:36,626
[SPEAKER_02]: Any notion I have in regard to it, that's second.

229
00:17:38,068 --> 00:17:40,771
[SPEAKER_02]: I am, identity, everything else.

230
00:17:43,118 --> 00:17:51,985
[SPEAKER_02]: But in order for that to be set into that right order, yeah, that attitude of yoniso, manasikāra, needs to be, like, needs to have been thoroughly developed.

231
00:17:52,386 --> 00:17:54,728
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, so it needs to become on the basic mode of attending.

232
00:17:54,848 --> 00:17:55,188
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

233
00:18:00,032 --> 00:18:03,515
[SPEAKER_03]: Outside of the precepts, the restraint, would you say also

234
00:18:06,129 --> 00:18:10,359
[SPEAKER_03]: I think you said it in one of the talks, just questioning yourself, like, who am I?

235
00:18:10,620 --> 00:18:11,723
[SPEAKER_03]: Type of, like, questioning.

236
00:18:12,284 --> 00:18:12,765
[SPEAKER_03]: What is this?

237
00:18:12,785 --> 00:18:13,287
[SPEAKER_03]: What's the...?

238
00:18:14,766 --> 00:18:22,931
[SPEAKER_03]: The meaning of this automatically forces you to head that direction, so to speak, like questioning...

239
00:18:22,991 --> 00:18:33,057
[SPEAKER_02]: I wouldn't say automatically, because it would force probably you, because all of the other things you know about it, and what you should need, what you need to be looking for.

240
00:18:33,658 --> 00:18:35,319
[SPEAKER_02]: So then it could probably result in that way.

241
00:18:35,339 --> 00:18:40,062
[SPEAKER_02]: But if you just say to somebody, just question yourself, it would sooner or later become mechanical.

242
00:18:40,892 --> 00:18:43,073
[SPEAKER_02]: Questions and... or mystical.

243
00:18:43,093 --> 00:18:51,278
[SPEAKER_02]: You just put yourself in a question and whatever experience comes out of it, and then the first thing that happens that's out of the ordinary will be the answer, whatever it might be.

244
00:18:52,979 --> 00:18:56,661
[SPEAKER_02]: But, yes, in principle, yes, the attitude of questioning is the attitude of mindfulness.

245
00:18:56,681 --> 00:18:58,122
[SPEAKER_02]: That's how you develop satipaṭṭhāna.

246
00:18:58,182 --> 00:19:02,725
[SPEAKER_02]: That's how you develop sati, through constant questioning and recollection.

247
00:19:04,746 --> 00:19:05,286
[SPEAKER_02]: What am I doing?

248
00:19:05,306 --> 00:19:05,727
[SPEAKER_02]: What am I doing?

249
00:19:06,746 --> 00:19:07,666
[SPEAKER_02]: Why am I doing it?

250
00:19:07,766 --> 00:19:08,427
[SPEAKER_02]: What's my...?"

251
00:19:08,487 --> 00:19:18,850
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, all of these questions, just to, again, shed light back onto the wider situation, so that you unabsorb yourself from whatever you ended up.

252
00:19:19,170 --> 00:19:25,853
[SPEAKER_02]: Because nature, the nature of the sense organs, and you might just to fall into that focus, that means going with the grain.

253
00:19:26,693 --> 00:19:31,195
[SPEAKER_02]: So, focusing, being absorbed, that's just following the grain.

254
00:19:32,155 --> 00:19:34,676
[SPEAKER_02]: And you want to basically go against that.

255
00:19:35,550 --> 00:19:41,312
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you can't just willingly unfocus, because that will become the object of your focus, new focus.

256
00:19:42,652 --> 00:19:47,874
[SPEAKER_02]: But then you realize, oh, but while you focus, you can learn to see that's the peripheral and that whole thing.

257
00:19:48,034 --> 00:19:55,356
[SPEAKER_02]: In other words, unfocus to the extent of whatever you've been focused at, without denying it, without trying to replace it.

258
00:19:59,417 --> 00:20:03,098
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's what's meant by, like, a reference point in satipaṭṭhāna, the practice.

259
00:20:04,227 --> 00:20:06,708
[SPEAKER_02]: And the whole thing of concurrent is the while.

260
00:20:07,048 --> 00:20:07,408
[SPEAKER_02]: Exactly.

261
00:20:07,428 --> 00:20:07,968
[SPEAKER_02]: That's what I mean.

262
00:20:08,188 --> 00:20:11,709
[SPEAKER_02]: Because if... And that's why I'm stressing the concurrency of it.

263
00:20:11,789 --> 00:20:22,973
[SPEAKER_02]: Because if I'm just unfocusing from this, without concurrently knowing that what I'm focusing is still there, I would just be replacing one focus... one thing I'm focused on with another thing I'm focused on.

264
00:20:23,473 --> 00:20:24,753
[SPEAKER_01]: And you're still freeing your mind a bit.

265
00:20:24,793 --> 00:20:25,053
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

266
00:20:25,073 --> 00:20:25,914
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the same direction.

267
00:20:25,934 --> 00:20:26,734
[SPEAKER_02]: Direction is not changing.

268
00:20:26,754 --> 00:20:27,714
[SPEAKER_02]: You're not going against the grain.

269
00:20:27,734 --> 00:20:30,035
[SPEAKER_02]: You're just changing the object of your focus.

270
00:20:31,888 --> 00:20:34,750
[SPEAKER_02]: But if you do it concurrently, you realize it's not about changing the object.

271
00:20:34,810 --> 00:20:37,211
[SPEAKER_02]: It's about discerning that peripheral to the object.

272
00:20:37,711 --> 00:20:40,752
[SPEAKER_02]: It's about learning how to put it in the brackets while it is there.

273
00:20:42,273 --> 00:20:43,814
[SPEAKER_02]: To see it to its own extent.

274
00:20:43,834 --> 00:20:54,159
[SPEAKER_02]: To see this phenomena independent of, as I said, the world, the space, the time, in that sense, the body, the I am, any notions, any concepts.

275
00:20:54,379 --> 00:20:57,661
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, this is first introduced there on its own to the way it is.

276
00:20:57,901 --> 00:20:58,261
[SPEAKER_02]: That's it.

277
00:21:00,363 --> 00:21:03,278
[SPEAKER_02]: And when you get used to that, that's... by the way, that's where the work starts.

278
00:21:04,630 --> 00:21:05,410
[SPEAKER_02]: That's not what it ends.

279
00:21:05,490 --> 00:21:05,850
[SPEAKER_02]: Okay.

280
00:21:05,890 --> 00:21:06,651
[SPEAKER_02]: Now, the way it is.

281
00:21:06,751 --> 00:21:07,951
[SPEAKER_02]: I saw things the way they are.

282
00:21:08,151 --> 00:21:08,451
[SPEAKER_02]: Okay.

283
00:21:08,491 --> 00:21:09,391
[SPEAKER_02]: So, what are you doing now?

284
00:21:09,911 --> 00:21:11,112
[SPEAKER_02]: Are you practicing what you think?

285
00:21:11,472 --> 00:21:12,492
[SPEAKER_02]: You're enlightened.

286
00:21:12,892 --> 00:21:13,752
[SPEAKER_02]: A thousand times more.

287
00:21:14,352 --> 00:21:14,733
[SPEAKER_02]: Exactly.

288
00:21:15,173 --> 00:21:15,773
[SPEAKER_02]: Now, you do it.

289
00:21:15,993 --> 00:21:18,794
[SPEAKER_02]: So, to discern the properties of that phenomenon.

290
00:21:18,994 --> 00:21:24,075
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why dhamma, the dhamma, is based on the word dhamma, which means things.

291
00:21:24,095 --> 00:21:25,215
[SPEAKER_02]: The nature of them.

292
00:21:25,515 --> 00:21:26,035
[SPEAKER_02]: Means things.

293
00:21:26,075 --> 00:21:26,675
[SPEAKER_02]: Means phenomena.

294
00:21:26,916 --> 00:21:28,896
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, the the mental objects are dhammas.

295
00:21:29,516 --> 00:21:29,816
[SPEAKER_02]: Thoughts.

296
00:21:30,797 --> 00:21:31,177
[SPEAKER_02]: That's it.

297
00:21:31,437 --> 00:21:32,317
[SPEAKER_02]: That's what dhammas are.

298
00:21:33,158 --> 00:21:35,059
[SPEAKER_02]: And you realize, yeah, everything is a phenomenon.

299
00:21:35,879 --> 00:21:44,944
[SPEAKER_02]: Physical phenomenon, mental phenomenon, everything has that aspect of an image, of an enduring image, phenomenal image, so to speak, phenomenological image.

300
00:21:45,764 --> 00:21:57,390
[SPEAKER_02]: So, understanding the characteristics of that, the nature of manifestation, the nature of anicca, dukkha, everything else in regard to its properties, oh, that's the way out, that's the dhamma.

301
00:21:59,531 --> 00:22:00,812
[SPEAKER_02]: So, yoniso, manasikāra,

302
00:22:01,625 --> 00:22:11,681
[SPEAKER_02]: Are you having dhammas available to be seen as dhammas, having discerned that, and the occurrence of another right instruction pointing certain things among those dhammas that you see now?

303
00:22:12,121 --> 00:22:16,388
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, yeah, you have the right view, because you have a basis for it, and you have the instruction as to what to see.

304
00:22:38,087 --> 00:22:45,271
[SPEAKER_04]: I find it difficult to regard the negative as a real thing, so to speak.

305
00:22:46,252 --> 00:22:47,133
[SPEAKER_02]: That's it.

306
00:22:47,453 --> 00:22:57,979
[SPEAKER_02]: See, anything less present or anything that's less in the center of your perception...

307
00:22:57,999 --> 00:22:58,319
[SPEAKER_02]: Exactly.

308
00:22:58,460 --> 00:22:59,980
[SPEAKER_02]: The attitude is a sort of attitude.

309
00:23:00,020 --> 00:23:02,002
[SPEAKER_02]: The word's attitude is, oh, this is less real.

310
00:23:03,500 --> 00:23:04,400
[SPEAKER_02]: That's exactly what I mean.

311
00:23:04,440 --> 00:23:08,021
[SPEAKER_02]: Learning how to see things to the extent they are real as such.

312
00:23:09,101 --> 00:23:10,021
[SPEAKER_02]: Less real than what?

313
00:23:12,882 --> 00:23:15,343
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you can actually question yourself in this regard.

314
00:23:15,643 --> 00:23:17,783
[SPEAKER_02]: See, you recognize, oh my God, this is less real.

315
00:23:19,163 --> 00:23:21,684
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you can't just, will you see, oh, I'll stop doing this.

316
00:23:22,484 --> 00:23:22,624
[SPEAKER_02]: No.

317
00:23:22,684 --> 00:23:24,985
[SPEAKER_02]: It's a result of wrongly attending to things.

318
00:23:25,045 --> 00:23:26,245
[SPEAKER_02]: But now that you spotted it,

319
00:23:26,946 --> 00:23:27,166
[SPEAKER_02]: Yep.

320
00:23:27,606 --> 00:23:29,347
[SPEAKER_02]: Reflect on it.

321
00:23:29,447 --> 00:23:31,008
[SPEAKER_02]: Why does this feel less real?

322
00:23:31,829 --> 00:23:32,689
[SPEAKER_02]: Less real to what?

323
00:23:33,190 --> 00:23:37,672
[SPEAKER_02]: But if it's less real, how can I talk about it in this real sense?

324
00:23:38,152 --> 00:23:39,113
[SPEAKER_02]: What am I thinking about?

325
00:23:39,233 --> 00:23:41,434
[SPEAKER_02]: All these thoughts are pretty real.

326
00:23:41,454 --> 00:23:42,415
[SPEAKER_02]: Feelings are pretty real.

327
00:23:42,475 --> 00:23:45,977
[SPEAKER_02]: So, less real becomes real as such.

328
00:23:46,737 --> 00:23:47,538
[SPEAKER_02]: See, that's the point.

329
00:23:48,098 --> 00:23:50,840
[SPEAKER_02]: The attitude of less real is not something you need to change.

330
00:23:51,460 --> 00:23:53,741
[SPEAKER_02]: You need to see that as a real phenomenon.

331
00:23:54,362 --> 00:23:56,283
[SPEAKER_02]: And then you stop regarding things as less real.

332
00:23:57,848 --> 00:24:10,456
[SPEAKER_04]: I think what I'm bumping into is maybe more along the lines of, you know, there's thought of the origin, but is that the origin that I need to be aware of?

333
00:24:10,496 --> 00:24:12,257
[SPEAKER_04]: Or is there something I need to search for?

334
00:24:12,317 --> 00:24:16,159
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, like, again, see, now, that question implies that the origin is outside of you.

335
00:24:16,659 --> 00:24:19,681
[SPEAKER_02]: So you're seeking this origin from where everything comes from.

336
00:24:19,721 --> 00:24:22,343
[SPEAKER_02]: You want to see the origin as an object, and that's impossible.

337
00:24:23,295 --> 00:24:27,078
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it's not the origin, even if it were the origin, because you're attending to it wrongly.

338
00:24:27,398 --> 00:24:28,299
[SPEAKER_02]: So, there's no origin there.

339
00:24:32,021 --> 00:24:33,742
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it's not like I have to stop that thought.

340
00:24:34,683 --> 00:24:37,465
[SPEAKER_02]: You need to see it real as such, and that's the origin.

341
00:24:38,186 --> 00:24:41,048
[SPEAKER_02]: So, origin is when you don't overshoot or undershoot.

342
00:24:42,348 --> 00:24:47,212
[SPEAKER_02]: Origin is when you attend to it rightly in the middle, or where it is, hence the middle way.

343
00:24:47,852 --> 00:24:49,954
[SPEAKER_02]: That's where the origin is.

344
00:24:50,034 --> 00:24:51,555
[SPEAKER_02]: You overshoot it, it's another object.

345
00:24:52,623 --> 00:24:54,424
[SPEAKER_02]: Of your focus, of your attention.

346
00:24:54,724 --> 00:24:55,245
[SPEAKER_02]: You understand?

347
00:24:55,285 --> 00:24:55,605
[SPEAKER_02]: It's the same.

348
00:24:55,985 --> 00:24:56,705
[SPEAKER_02]: It's another object.

349
00:24:59,707 --> 00:25:03,049
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you find the origin not by... well, you attend with the origin.

350
00:25:03,189 --> 00:25:04,970
[SPEAKER_02]: You learn how to attend concurrently.

351
00:25:06,331 --> 00:25:07,612
[SPEAKER_02]: Not before or after.

352
00:25:10,353 --> 00:25:17,998
[SPEAKER_02]: So, concurrent attention is what the yoniso manasikāra is for you at the time, even if you don't think that's what the yoniso manasikāra is.

353
00:25:18,638 --> 00:25:20,319
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why it needs repetition and training.

354
00:25:20,937 --> 00:25:22,938
[SPEAKER_02]: Not just an intellectual answer.

355
00:25:22,958 --> 00:25:32,485
[SPEAKER_03]: I was going to say, practically speaking, you're probably going to have to do the... where you get sucked in, and then you step back out.

356
00:25:32,505 --> 00:25:32,605
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

357
00:25:32,645 --> 00:25:33,286
[SPEAKER_03]: Get sucked in.

358
00:25:33,326 --> 00:25:33,786
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, of course.

359
00:25:33,906 --> 00:25:35,968
[SPEAKER_03]: That you need that pushing back.

360
00:25:36,108 --> 00:25:38,630
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why the precepts and guiding of the sense door is everything.

361
00:25:38,730 --> 00:25:46,475
[SPEAKER_02]: It's just... it's there like a container, a gate that keeps you from, like, when you're sucked in, from, like, going too far.

362
00:25:46,555 --> 00:25:47,036
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

363
00:25:48,108 --> 00:25:50,569
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's never optional.

364
00:25:50,589 --> 00:25:52,749
[SPEAKER_00]: The body isn't there.

365
00:25:52,789 --> 00:25:53,229
[SPEAKER_02]: Exactly.

366
00:25:53,309 --> 00:25:54,069
[SPEAKER_02]: Within a feeling.

367
00:25:54,269 --> 00:26:00,350
[SPEAKER_02]: Those understandings, those clarities, would realize, oh, whether actually I tend to describe it or not, body is there.

368
00:26:00,811 --> 00:26:02,351
[SPEAKER_02]: Always does itself, always.

369
00:26:02,371 --> 00:26:04,251
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, yeah.

370
00:26:04,331 --> 00:26:10,493
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's where you can become less involved, so to speak, with seeing things as phenomena and learning how to think rightly.

371
00:26:10,993 --> 00:26:12,453
[SPEAKER_02]: In other words, knowledge is taking over.

372
00:26:13,484 --> 00:26:17,126
[SPEAKER_02]: So, mindfulness is resulting in certain perceptions becoming set now.

373
00:26:17,766 --> 00:26:23,269
[SPEAKER_02]: So, even if... That's how Sotāpanna can become careless and can proliferate his sensuality and so on.

374
00:26:24,249 --> 00:26:26,770
[SPEAKER_02]: But he cannot not see things as phenomena, fundamentally.

375
00:26:27,711 --> 00:26:30,052
